#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083042 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "385b1ccd-ea68-5c59-a0e6-1a0bb7937460" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/id #uuid "edbd084f-e8ad-53e7-9384-94094a6e7bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5879" :question/problem "Solve $2x^3+3x^2-8x-12=0$." :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{3} + 3x^{2} - 8x - 12 &=  \\\\\n(2x^{3} + 3x^{2}) - (8x + 12) &=  \\\\\nx^{2}(2x + 3) - 4(2x + 3) &=  \\\\\n(2x + 3) \\cdot x^{2} - (2x + 3) \\cdot 4 &=  \\\\\n&= (2x + 3)(x^{2} - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(x^2-4) = 0\n$$\n\nby setting each factor equal to $0$.\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x+3)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$2x + 3|= 0; 2x|=-3; x|=-\\frac{3}{2}$.\n- Setting the factor $(x^2-4)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$x^{2} - 4|= 0; x^{2}|= 4; x|= ± 2$.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-2, \\; -\\dfrac{3}{2}, \\; 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083043 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6158975-c81e-5621-aa39-3f2dc728f107" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Manipulating the Equation and Then Grouping"}] :question/id #uuid "90296377-6785-5b6d-87ca-8a88d4b266c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5880" :question/problem "Solve $8x^3+3=4x^2+6x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we subtract $4x^2$ and $6x$ from both sides to make the right side $0$. To prepare for factoring by grouping, we make sure to write the resulting cubic in standard form.\n\n$$\n8x^3-4x^2-6x+3=0\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x^{3} - 4x^{2} - 6x + 3 &=  \\\\\n(8x^{3} - 4x^{2}) - (6x - 3) &=  \\\\\n4x^{2}(2x - 1) - 3(2x - 1) &=  \\\\\n(2x - 1) \\cdot 4x^{2} - (2x - 1) \\cdot 3 &=  \\\\\n&= (2x - 1)(4x^{2} - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve.\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x-1)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$2x - 1|= 0; 2x|= 1; x|= \\frac{1}{2}$.\n- Setting the factor $(4x^2-3)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$4x^{2} - 3|= 0; 4x^{2}|= 3; x^{2}|= \\frac{3}{4}; x|= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}}; x|= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}, \\; \\dfrac12, \\; \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083067 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7b17a61-b225-5493-98b0-58d15217aa31" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Grouping"}] :question/id #uuid "1c104a42-2eea-5029-8ada-7d3bb90add52" :question/math-academy-id "e-6110" :question/problem "Solve $x^3+x^2-9x-9=0$." :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} + x^{2} - 9x - 9 &=  \\\\\n(x^{3} + x^{2}) - (9x + 9) &=  \\\\\nx^{2}(x + 1) - 9(x + 1) &=  \\\\\n(x + 1) \\cdot x^{2} - (x + 1) \\cdot 9 &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 1)(x^{2} - 9)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x^2-9) = 0\n$$\n\nby setting each factor equal to $0$.\n\n- Setting the factor $(x+1)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$x + 1|= 0; x|=-1$.\n- Setting the factor $(x^2-9)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$x^{2} - 9|= 0; x^{2}|= 9; x|= ± 3$.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\, \\pm 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083068 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01d58dfd-315a-5a4f-ae16-1db04010169a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Negative Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/id #uuid "9d7a8d1c-449e-5e58-971f-7a063d52a703" :question/math-academy-id "e-6111" :question/problem "Solve $-x^3-4x^2+5x+20=0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\nx^3+4x^2-5x-20=0\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} + 4x^{2} - 5x - 20 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2}(x + 4) - 5(x + 4) &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 5)(x + 4) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve\n\n$$\n(x^2-5)(x+4)=0\n$$\n\nby setting each factor equal to $0$.\n\n- Setting the factor $(x^2-5)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$x^{2}|= 5; x|= ± \\sqrt{5}$.\n- Setting the factor $(x+4)$ equal to $0$, we get\n$x|=-4$.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-4, \\pm \\sqrt 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7b17a61-b225-5493-98b0-58d15217aa31" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Grouping"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "978fe695-4870-5063-84c2-77d196d63866" :question/math-academy-id "q-114145" :question/problem "Solve ${m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}-3{m}^{2})-(2m-6) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m-3)-2(m-3) & = \\\\ (m-3)\\cdot {m}^{2}-(m-3)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (m-3)({m}^{2}-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m-3)({m}^{2}-2)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m-3 & =0 \\\\ m & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({m}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ {m}^{2} & =2 \\\\ m & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083247 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6158975-c81e-5621-aa39-3f2dc728f107" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Manipulating the Equation and Then Grouping"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4ef63b9-4994-5aab-8c0b-2be94f67268a" :question/math-academy-id "q-114339" :question/problem "Solve $8{u}^{3}-6{u}^{2}-72u+54=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27 & = \\\\ (4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2})-(36u-27) & = \\\\ {u}^{2}(4u-3)-9(4u-3) & = \\\\ (4u-3)\\cdot {u}^{2}-(4u-3)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (4u-3)({u}^{2}-9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(4u-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-3 & =0 \\\\ 4u & =3 \\\\ u & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({u}^{2}-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2} & =9 \\\\ u & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $\\frac{3}{4}$ and $\\pm 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083246 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "385b1ccd-ea68-5c59-a0e6-1a0bb7937460" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9865aa6e-bb17-5d0e-8247-1f49bdc82814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{5}{6}, ± \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080088} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb34ae4-aacb-524b-a6fb-da9fb454b34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{6}, ± \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080089} {:answer/id #uuid "a87fe441-668c-5cab-ab56-a073e7a67b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{4}{3}, ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}}" :